        {
          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"stendhal-le-rouge-et-le-noir-08929319066ffa4b\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":1,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.96,\"analysis\":\"Mathilde directly avows as true the cessation of her love: “Je ne vous aime plus.” Its dominant force is truth-conditional, reinforced by her explicit claim to sincerity and her explanation that her imagination deceived her. In context it also helps effect a rejection or rupture, giving slight performative support, but it does not chiefly undertake a commitment. Nothing presents the words as a reflex-like affective discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"Mathilde’s pride, anger, and recent contrary feelings complicate the durability and motivation of the avowal, but the narrator confirms that at this moment she genuinely feels no love. The only material classification uncertainty is whether the rejection function merits more performative weight.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Mathilde says “Je ne vous aime plus, monsieur” directly to Julien during a private confrontation in the library.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Mathilde presents the statement as a sincere correction of her earlier passion and uses it to reject Julien and deny his power over her. Her avowal is shaped by wounded pride, class contempt, remorse, and hostility; she subsequently attacks Julien with deliberately rehearsed cruelties. Nevertheless, the narration says that at this moment she truly no longer feels love, while also suggesting that her state may be unstable rather than a settled, lasting self-knowledge.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“ce danger ... ne m'empêchera certainement pas d'être sincère. Je ne vous aime plus, monsieur, mon imagination folle m'a trompée”\",\"supports\":\"Her explicit appeal to sincerity and claim that her imagination deceived her strongly support a truth-conditional avowal of ceased love.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Elle ne faisait que répéter ce que depuis huit jours, disait dans son coeur l'avocat du parti contraire à l'amour.”\",\"supports\":\"The words arise from an internally developed case against love rather than an unmediated, reflex-like verbal discharge, supporting E=0.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“elle avait donc pu rompre à tout jamais” and “il n'a et n'aura jamais aucun empire sur moi”\",\"supports\":\"Mathilde understands the encounter as a decisive rupture and repudiation of Julien's relational power, supplying limited performative support.\",\"confidence\":0.92},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Elle était si heureuse que réellement elle n'avait plus d'amour en ce moment.”\",\"supports\":\"The narrator confirms the truth of her present lack of love, while the temporal qualification “en ce moment” indicates possible instability.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is marked as a present-tense, negative cessative construction with the temporal modifier “no_longer.”\",\"supports\":\"Confirms that the core form presents the cessation of a previously existing loving state.\",\"confidence\":0.99}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the avowal of a ceased emotional state, while a small P score registers its contextual role in effecting rejection. Intensity, pride, and cruelty are contextual features and create no need for E or O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":\"The negative, cessative wording remains an explicit love-state avowal for purposes of the compact ontology.\"}"
        }
      ],
